- How would you optimize memory for large, sparse alphabets?
- How would you extend to support deletion of all instances of a word efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with duplicates using a compact, well-known DS (Fenwick). Requires handling inserts, deletes, and k-th retrieval within tight bounds.

## Key Concepts

- Coordinate compression to bound domain size
- Fenwick Tree for prefix sums (counts)
- getKthSmallest via binary lifting on Fenwick
- Handling duplicates and safe deletes
- Time: O(log m) per operation, m distinct values

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.f=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  add(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta; }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){
    let idx=0; let bit=1;
    while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit <<= 1;
    for(let step=bit; step>0; step>>=1){
      const t = idx+step;
      if(t<=this.n && this.f[t] < k){
        idx = t;
        k -= this.f[t];
      }
    }
    return idx+1;
  }
}
class KthMultiset {
  constructor(vals){
    this.values = Array.from(new Set(vals)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    this.index = new Map(this.values.map((v,i)=>[v,i+1]));
    this.ft = new Fenwick(this.values.length);
  }
  add(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, 1); }
  remove(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, -1); }
  getKthSmallest(k){ const idx = this.ft.kth(k); return this.values[idx-1]; }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical streaming component with O(1) amortized updates. Uses a monotonic deque for min and a running sum for quick statistics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window
- Monotonic deque
- Amortized analysis
- Edge cases when window not full

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindow {
  constructor(w){
    this.w = w;
    this.q = []; // {val, idx}
    this.min = []; // {val, idx}
    this.sum = 0;
    this.i = 0;
  }
  add(v){
    const idx = this.i++;
    this.q.push({val:v, idx});
    this.sum += v;
    while (this.min.length && this.min[this.min.length-1].val >= v) this.min.pop();
    this.min.push({val:v, idx});
    if (this.q.length > this.w){
      const old = this.q.shift();
      this.sum -= old.val;
      if (this.min[0].idx === old.idx) this.min.shift();
    }
  }
  getMin(){ return this.min[0]?.val; }
  getSum(){ return this.sum; }
  getAverage(){ return this.q.length ? this.sum / this.q.length : undefined; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if w changes over time?
- How do you handle duplicates and negative values?

